Releases:
  release cut [<label>|auto] [--pre <id>] [--at <sha>] [--yes] [--build <command>] [--param <k=v>]
              [--params-file <file>] [--src <dir>] [-o <file>] [--lexicon <name>]
              [--actor <name>] [--sign [<key file>]] [--channel <tag>]
              [--lane <name>] [--override-green]
              [--image <component>=<repository>@sha256:<hex>] [--json]
                        A dry run of a cut of this member from HEAD, which
                        must be clean, on trunk and pushed, and carry a
                        ci/green tag: compile every environment in the
                        config's environments and print what the cut would
                        build, record and tag, and what publishes where.
                        --yes cuts: builds the artifacts once, stores the
                        release on chant/lifecycle under its digest, records
                        the actor and run id, tags the commit with the tag
                        release.tag names (default release/<label>) here,
                        and prints the next command.
                        --build runs before each environment's templates are
                        read, with CHANT_ENV set; {env} in --params-file is
                        replaced by the environment. --sign signs the cut with
                        the actor's ssh key; an identity.gates release entry
                        requires it. A project with no components releases
                        what chant build --env <env> writes: --src <dir> is
                        the build root, -o <file> the output (dist/build.yaml),
                        --lexicon narrows it. --at <sha> cuts that commit of
                        trunk from a temporary worktree instead of HEAD, so
                        the checkout need not be clean. The label reaches
                        every compile and build as CHANT_RELEASE_LABEL.
                        --image, repeatable, hands the cut a component's image
                        built elsewhere, by digest: the release records it and
                        the cut builds nothing for that component (#4694).
                        A green commit of a pushed chant/compose/<name>
                        branch is cut too, recording its base and sources.
                        --channel <tag> records the tag a publish moves in
                        place of latest. --lane <name> (or release.lane in
                        chant.config.ts) checks a ci.green lane's tag,
                        ci/green-<lane>/<sha>, in place of ci/green.
                        --override-green cuts a commit that is not green
                        under a person's override, recorded with chant
                        approve policy-override release-<sha12>; the cut
                        spends it and records who and why.
                        auto cuts the label release next computes, and every
                        labelled cut records the intent files it consumed.
  release compose <name> [--base <label|sha>] (--pr <n> | --work <id>)...
                  [--yes] [--json]
                        Cherry-pick the chosen trunk changes, in trunk order,
                        onto the base, each with a Chant-Composed-From
                        trailer, on chant/compose/<name>. Appends to a branch
                        that exists; stops on a conflict, naming the change
                        and files, and writes nothing. A dry run unless --yes,
                        which moves the branch and pushes it.
  release candidates [--env <env>] [--lane <name>] [--forge-issues] [--json]
                        The green commits of trunk a release can be cut from,
                        newest first, each with the commits, work items, pull
                        requests and flags it would ship, and what each
                        environment runs. --lane reads a lane's green tags,
                        as release cut --lane checks them. --forge-issues asks
                        the forge which issues each pull request closes, once
                        per pull request
  release publish <label|digest> [--yes] [--allow-unsigned]
                  [--require-provenance] [--json]
                        Publish a cut release to the targets release.publish
                        in chant.config.ts declares (npm, OCI registries,
                        forge release assets), each with the release's
                        provenance and an SBOM when it has a source. A dry
                        run without --yes. Refuses a revoked release, and
                        with a target a checkout that is not the release's
                        commit. Under security verified-releases it refuses
                        one whose signature does not verify against
                        .chant/allowed_signers unless --allow-unsigned; under
                        supply-chain it warns when the run has no OIDC token
                        and refuses a target with no SBOM source.
                        --require-provenance refuses a run with no OIDC.
  release verify <label|digest> [--sigstore [--assets <dir>]] [--json]
                        Check, offline, that the release was signed by a key
                        .chant/allowed_signers lists at the release's commit.
                        --sigstore also checks the Sigstore signatures and
                        SLSA provenance the publish recorded, with cosign:
                        an image's in its registry, a forge release's in the
                        directory --assets names.
  release images <label|digest> [--no-build] [-o <file>] [--json]
                        Build the images release.images declares again from
                        the release's commit, refuse one whose digest is not
                        the recorded one, and write the recorded digests to
                        the file CHANT_RELEASE_DIGESTS names for the build.
  release doctor [--json]
                        Before a cut, ask each release.publish target whether
                        a publish from here can succeed: credentials, OIDC,
                        npm trusted publishers and dist-tags, registry and
                        forge access. Says what it can't observe from here.
                        Exits 1 when a check fails.
  release artifacts <label|digest> [--no-build] [--from <dir>] [-o <dir>] [--json]
                        Build the files release.artifacts declares again from
                        the release's commit, or check those on disk
                        (--no-build) or copied from <dir> (--from), and
                        refuse one whose digest is not the recorded one. -o
                        stages the checked files in <dir> for the next job.
  release stamp <label> [--train <id|digest>] [--json]
                        Write the label as the version of each package.json
                        release.version names, and the lockfile's entries for
                        them, as the cut does before its build. The publish
                        job runs it before its build. With ranges set in
                        release.version, each workspace dependency's range
                        takes the label its package has; --train gives the
                        labels of the other members' packages from a train.
  release tag <label|digest|tag> [--json]
                        The tag release.tag gives a release of this member
                        (default release/<label>), found by its label, its
                        digest or the tag itself, with its floating tag.
  release init [--yes] [--force] [--forge github|gitlab|forgejo] [--json]
                        Set up chant release in a project: detect its packages,
                        images and tags, write release in chant.config.ts and
                        the release workflow, and list the registry steps to
                        take by hand. A dry run unless --yes; --force replaces
                        an existing release block.
  release workflow [--forge github|gitlab|forgejo] [--install <command>]
              [--build <command>] [--chant <command>] [--output <file>]
                        Write the publish workflow, run on the tags
                        release.tag makes (default release/**).
                        Under supply-chain it passes --require-provenance, and
                        under verified-releases it runs release verify first
  release next [--at <sha>] [--pre <id>] [--json]
                        The label of this member's next release, from the
                        conventional commits since its previous release and
                        the .chant/release/*.md intent files no cut consumed.
                        --pre <id> makes it a prerelease, <version>-<id>.<n>.
  release changed [--at <sha>] [--bump-member <member>=<level>]... [--json]
                        The members to release at a commit, in publish order,
                        each with why: changed, needed-by <member>,
                        range-broken-by <member> or intent-pending, and the
                        bump the commits and intent files give, unless
                        --bump-member sets it. Reads local refs only
  release list [--json]  Every release of this member with its targets, its
                        cuts and the environments that run it.
  release export <label|digest> --env <env> [-o <dir>] [--json]
                        Write the files the release recorded for <env>, each
                        checked against its digest, at their recorded paths
                        under <dir> (default: here), for a deploy to read.
  release deprecate|yank|restore <label|digest> [--yes] [--reason <text>]
                        [--replacement <label>] [--forge-release prerelease|draft|delete]
                        [--actor <name>] [--sign [<key file>]] [--json]
                        Record a status for a published release and act on
                        each target that can: deprecate warns npm users; yank
                        also moves npm's dist-tag and the OCI moving tags back
                        to the previous active release, withdraws the forge
                        release and reverts a tap's formula; restore undoes
                        it. A dry run unless --yes; resumes like publish.

